/* book Worked example 2: Q: The same two-span beam (AB = 6 m, BC = 4 m, simple ends A and C) now carries a 40 kN point load at the middle of AB and a 30 kN point load at the middle of BC. Find MB, the reactions and the largest sagging moments.
   1. Right side for central point loads: −(3P₁L₁² + 3P₂L₂²)/8 = −(3 × 40 × 36 + 3 × 30 × 16)/8 = −(4320 + 1440)/8 = −720.
   2. Left side with MA = MC = 0: 2MB(6 + 4) = 20MB. So MB = −720/20 = −36 kN·m (hogging).
   3. Span AB as a free body: RA = 40/2 + MB/6 = 20 − 6 = 14 kN; the B end takes 20 + 6 = 26 kN.
   4. Span BC: RC = 30/2 + MB/4 = 15 − 9 = 6 kN; the B end takes 15 + 9 = 24 kN. RB = 26 + 24 = 50 kN. Check: 14 + 50 + 6 = 70 kN.
   5. Sagging under the loads: AB at 3 m, M = 14 × 3 = 42 kN·m; BC at 2 m from C, M = 6 × 2 = 12 kN·m. The free moments PL/4 were 60 and 30 kN·m; half of MB (18) comes off each.
   Answer: MB = −36 kN·m; reactions 14, 50 and 6 kN; largest sagging moment 42 kN·m under the 40 kN load. */
